    "summary": "Detailed exploration of cognitive interaction models, gulf bridging, error taxonomy, Norman's core design heuristics, and Gestalt perceptual laws.",
    "content": "\n# Unit 4: Interaction Design Principles & Models\n\n## 1. Donald Norman’s 7 Stages of Action\nDonald Norman modeled human interaction with machines as a cycle transitioning between cognitive mental states and the physical system:\n\n```\n                         [ GOAL ]\n                   Forming the Goal\n                     /                   [ GULF OF EXECUTION ]   [ GULF OF EVALUATION ]\n                   /              1. Intention to act                5. Perceive system state\n2. Action sequence specification   6. Interpret perceived state\n3. Executing action sequence       7. Evaluate state against goal\n                                 /\n                 [ PHYSICAL SYSTEM STATE ]\n```\n\n### Bridging the Two Gulfs:\n* **Gulf of Execution:** The psychological distance between the user's formulated goal and the actions required by the system.\n  * *Bridged by:* Clear affordances, obvious signifiers, intuitive controls, natural mapping, and constrained input options.\n* **Gulf of Evaluation:** The cognitive effort required to assess the system's current state and determine whether the goal was satisfied.\n  * *Bridged by:* Immediate visual feedback, transparent state displays, meaningful confirmations, and sound/tactile responses.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Norman’s Error Typology: Slips vs. Mistakes\n\n| Attribute | Slips | Mistakes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Origin** | Execution failure | Goal formulation or planning failure |\n| **Mental Model** | Correct mental model and goal | Flawed mental model or incorrect understanding |\n| **Mechanism** | Subconscious automated action diverted (e.g., clicking wrong button, typo) | User establishes the wrong goal and executes the wrong sequence deliberately |\n| **Typical User** | Experienced users performing routine tasks | Novice or intermittent users dealing with confusing systems |\n| **Design Remedy** | Defensive UI constraints, confirmation dialogues, undo/redo emergency exits | Improved conceptual clarity, transparent state, guidance, and education |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Norman’s 7 Fundamental Principles of Design\n1. **Use Both Knowledge in the World and Knowledge in the Head:** Externalize information in visual interfaces (menus, labels) so users do not rely solely on memory.\n2. **Simplify the Structure of Tasks:** Keep task sequences concise; prevent deep nested menu hierarchies.\n3. **Make Things Visible:** Bridge execution and evaluation by ensuring options, controls, and states are visually obvious.\n4. **Get the Mappings Right:** Ensure natural, intuitive geometric correspondence between controls and their real-world effects (e.g., stove burner knobs).\n5. **Exploit Affordances and Constraints:** Use physical, logical, semantic, and cultural constraints so wrong actions are physically impossible or intuitively unnatural.\n6. **Design for Error:** Assume errors will occur. Provide robust recovery pathways, undo operations, and clear non-punitive error messages.\n7. **When All Else Fails, Standardize:** If natural mappings cannot be found, establish consistent arbitrary standards (e.g., traffic light colors).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Affordances, Signifiers & Constraints\n* **Affordance (J.J. Gibson 1977, Norman 1988):** The actionable properties between an object and an actor (e.g., a chair affords sitting, a button affords pressing).\n* **Signifier:** Any visual or auditory cue that indicates *where* and *how* an action should take place (e.g., a raised bevel on a button, a door pull handle).\n* **Constraints:**\n  * *Physical Constraints:* Physical boundaries prevent incorrect input (e.g., 3-prong electrical plug).\n  * *Semantic Constraints:* Meaning of the situation governs valid choices.\n  * *Cultural Constraints:* Conventions learned within a society (e.g., red = stop, green = go).\n  * *Logical Constraints:* Unambiguous spatial reasoning (e.g., two parts remain, two holes remain).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Gestalt Principles of Perceptual Organization\nDiscovered by Max Wertheimer, Kurt Koffka, and Wolfgang Köhler:\n* **Proximity:** Elements placed close together are perceived as belonging to a collective group.\n* **Similarity:** Elements sharing visual properties (color, shape, size) are perceived as related.\n* **Continuity:** The human eye follows continuous lines and curves rather than abrupt changes.\n* **Closure:** The brain supplies missing information to perceive incomplete shapes as whole objects.\n* **Figure-Ground:** The eye separates visual fields into focal figures and ambient background.\n* **Common Fate:** Elements that move in the same direction at the same velocity are perceived as a unit.\n\n\n---\n\n## 5. Prototype Fidelity & Scope: Horizontal vs. Vertical Prototypes\nPrototypes differ in both visual fidelity and functional depth:\n\n* **Horizontal Prototype:** Depicts the **entire interface at a broad surface level** without implementing underlying backend logic or functional depth. Users can navigate across screens, but actual database operations or calculations are not executed.\n* **Vertical Prototype:** Implements a **narrow slice of complete, deep functionality** down through all architectural layers (UI, business logic, database).\n* **Low-Fidelity Prototypes:** Sketched paper mock-ups, index cards, and storyboards created quickly to explore early design concepts without code.\n* **Wizard of Oz Technique:** A specialized prototyping method where a user interacts with an apparently automated system that is secretly operated behind the scenes by a hidden human operator.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Cognitive Walkthrough Evaluation Process\n* **Team Composition:** A standard cognitive walkthrough inspection team typically requires **3 to 5 expert evaluators**.\n* **Methodology:** Evaluators simulate the step-by-step cognitive problem-solving process of a user performing realistic task scenarios.\n* **Form of Evaluation:** Evaluators do NOT merely give binary 'Yes' or 'No' answers; they must provide structured, detailed qualitative justifications for each of the 4 core walkthrough questions to pinpoint potential user failures.\n"
