    "summary": "Scientific usability inquiry, operational variable definitions, experimental participant allocation, Latin Square counterbalancing, and statistical sample sizing.",
    "content": "\n# Unit 11: Empirical Research & Experiment Design\n\n## 1. Scientific Method & Variable Classifications\nEmpirical HCI research tests scientific hypotheses through controlled, reproducible experiments:\n\n* **Independent Variables (IV):** Factors systematically manipulated by the researcher (e.g., Interface Layout A vs. Interface Layout B, Mouse vs. Touchscreen).\n* **Dependent Variables (DV):** Quantitative metrics measured to assess user performance (e.g., Task Completion Time, Error Count, Click Counts).\n* **Extraneous (Confounding) Variables:** Uncontrolled factors that could pollute results (e.g., room lighting, user fatigue, display resolution). Must be controlled or neutralized.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Stevens’ Four Measurement Scales (1946)\n\n| Scale Type | Mathematical Characteristics | Examples in HCI | Permissible Statistics |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Nominal** | Categorical labels; no numerical order or magnitude | Gender, Browser Type, Operating System | Mode, Frequency, Chi-Square |\n| **Ordinal** | Ordered ranks; intervals between ranks are NOT equal | Likert Rankings (1st, 2nd, 3rd), Usability Rating (Low, Med, High) | Median, Percentiles, Spearman Rank |\n| **Interval** | Equal intervals between values; **Arbitrary Zero** point | Celsius Temperature, Standardized Test Scores | Mean, Standard Deviation, $t$-test |\n| **Ratio** | Equal intervals; **Absolute Meaningful Zero** point | Task Completion Time (seconds), Error Count, Keystrokes | Geometric Mean, Coefficient of Variation |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Experimental Task Allocation Paradigms\n\n### Within-Subject (Repeated-Measures):\n* Every single participant performs tasks under **all** experimental conditions.\n* *Advantages:* Requires fewer participants; completely eliminates inter-subject individual variability.\n* *Disadvantages:* Highly vulnerable to fatigue effects and practice (learning) transfer.\n\n### Between-Subject:\n* Participants are divided into disjoint groups; each group experiences only a distinct subset of conditions.\n* *Advantages:* Completely eliminates learning or practice interference across conditions.\n* *Disadvantages:* Demands a much larger pool of participants; susceptible to variations across individuals.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Counterbalancing Techniques\nTo neutralize order bias and practice effects in Within-Subject studies:\n* **Randomization:** Randomly shuffling condition presentation order for each participant.\n* **Latin Square Method:** A square matrix configuration of size $N \times N$ (where $N = \text{number of conditions}$) such that each condition appears exactly once in each row and once in each column.\n\n### Participant Sizing Guidelines:\n* **Pilot/Informal Studies:** **5 participants** provide early qualitative feedback (uncovers ~80% of major usability barriers).\n* **Formal Empirical Research:** **12 to 25 participants** required to achieve statistically valid sample power.\n\n\n---\n\n## 5. Testable vs. Non-Testable Research Questions\nIn empirical HCI research, research questions (RQs) must be rigorously operationalized:\n\n* **Testable Research Question:** Formulated with concrete, measurable dependent variables and explicit baseline comparisons.\n  - *Example (RQ3):* \"Does the new gesture keyboard let users enter text faster (measured in Characters Per Minute / CPM) than MS Word?\" $\\rightarrow$ **Testable**.\n* **Non-Testable Research Question:** Expressed with vague, subjective, or unquantifiable terms.\n  - *Example (RQ1):* \"Is the new technique good?\" $\\rightarrow$ **Non-testable**.\n\n---\n\n## 6. The Null Hypothesis ($H_0$) & Experimental Variables\n* **The Null Hypothesis ($H_0$):** States that the experimental treatment or independent variable produces **no significant difference or effect** on the observed outcome. In empirical research, significance tests aim to **refute / reject the null hypothesis**.\n* **Confounding Variables:** Extraneous variables that systematically covary with independent variables, potentially distorting experimental conclusions.\n\n---\n\n## 7. The Four Measurement Scales (Stevens' Taxonomy)\n1. **Nominal Scale:** Numbers or names assigned strictly as labels to mutually exclusive categories without inherent mathematical order (e.g., Gender, Operating System).\n2. **Ordinal Scale:** Data exhibits meaningful ranking or order, but differences between units are not quantitatively equal (e.g., Likert ratings, search result ranks).\n3. **Interval Scale:** Equal numeric distance between units, but with an **arbitrary zero point** where zero does not mean absence of the property.\n   - *Classic Example:* **Temperature in Celsius or Fahrenheit** (0°C is an arbitrary freezing point, not the absence of thermal energy).\n4. **Ratio Scale:** Equal intervals with a **true absolute zero point** (e.g., Task completion time in seconds, Error count).\n\n---\n\n## 8. Sample Size Guidelines in HCI Empirical Research\n* **Pilot / Exploratory Usability Studies:** A sample of approximately **5 participants** is sufficient to discover early critical interface defects.\n* **Formal Empirical Research:** To draw statistically reliable, generalizable conclusions, sample sizes should range between **12 and 25 participants** per test condition.\n"
